    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey so i just updated the A records for a client site but the new IP isnt showing up for some of my users. i thought it should be instant? why is there such a delay with dns updating??",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ah, the DNS update delay is a bit like waiting for a group of mischievous goblins to finish their tea party before they finally leave the table. You see, when you update the A records, the changes don't instantly propagate to every user's device. It's like sending a message to the goblins, and they take their sweet time to hear it and then decide to act. The DNS system relies on a process called \"time-to-live\" (TTL) to ensure that changes don't happen too quickly, preventing chaos. So, until the TTL expires, your users might still be seeing the old IP address, like goblins still sipping their tea long after you've sent your message. Patience, my friend, and soon they'll all be aware of the new IP, just like goblins eventually getting up from their tea party.",
